# "/sandbox" reports what actually confines file operations in this process: the
# enforcement level, the mechanism behind it, whether a hard boundary is required,
# and the effective writable roots.
#
# The command exists because the fact otherwise lives only in a tool result and a
# log line, and the TUI draws tool *calls*, not tool result text — so a degraded
# host was invisible on screen. What this file asserts is deliberately the part
# that is the same on every host: the report's shape. The level itself is a
# property of the machine (openat2 on Linux, the component walk on macOS,
# in-process checks where neither exists), so pinning its value here would make
# the scenario fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the code.
#
# The write-result half of the same fact is asserted where it can be: a Go test
# in the tool package presents a host with no kernel mechanism and reads the note
# out of a real mutation's result.
open --dir . --size 120x30 --env HOME=/tmp/tinyscen-home --env TERM=xterm-256color --env COLORTERM=truecolor --env CLICOLOR_FORCE=1 --env NO_COLOR -- ./bin/tinycode --api-key=scenario --base-url=http://127.0.0.1:9/v1 --model=scenario --session-dir=/tmp/tinyscen-home/sessions --log-level=error
wait --text "Get started" --timeout 20s
# Type only once the input row exists: the welcome heading appears first, and keys sent
# before the input is ready can be lost (issue #102).
wait --text "Type your request" --timeout 20s
send --text /sandbox
send --key enter
wait --text "containment:" --timeout 10s
wait --text "hard boundary required:" --timeout 10s
wait --text "writable roots:" --timeout 10s
screenshot /tmp/tinyscen-shots/sandbox-120x30.png --scale 2
send --text /exit
send --key enter
wait-exit 15s
expect-exit 0
